Last weekend, I visited a family restaurant called Spice Garden with three friends. The restaurant serves a mixture of South Indian and North Indian food. We selected it because everyone in the group had different food preferences.

The restaurant was located on a busy road, but valet parking was available. The entrance was clean and well maintained. We had not made a reservation, so we waited for about fifteen minutes before getting a table.

The dining area was spacious and comfortable. The lighting was warm, and the background music was quiet enough for conversation. The restaurant was almost full, but it did not feel extremely noisy.

We started with paneer tikka and pepper chicken. The paneer tikka was soft and had a mild smoky flavor. The seasoning was balanced and not overly spicy. The pepper chicken had a much stronger flavor. It contained black pepper, curry leaves, and onions. I preferred the pepper chicken because it was spicy without being excessively oily.

For the main course, we ordered butter chicken, dal makhani, garlic naan, vegetable biryani, and gongura chicken with steamed rice.

The butter chicken had a smooth and creamy tomato gravy. It tasted slightly sweet, which two members of our group liked, although I personally would have preferred a little more spice.

The dal makhani was one of the best dishes. The lentils were creamy and had a mild smoky flavor. It went particularly well with the garlic naan.

The gongura chicken was very different from the North Indian dishes. It had a sour and spicy taste because of the gongura leaves. The chicken was tender, and the curry worked well with plain steamed rice.

The vegetable biryani was good but not exceptional. The rice was properly cooked and aromatic, but the flavor was milder than we expected.

Service was generally good. The staff were polite and regularly refilled water. However, the main course took nearly thirty minutes to arrive because the restaurant was crowded.

For dessert, we ordered gulab jamun and filter coffee. The gulab jamun was served warm and was soft inside. The filter coffee was strong and enjoyable, although it contained slightly more sugar than I normally prefer.

The total bill was a little higher than a regular neighborhood restaurant, but the portion sizes were generous enough for sharing.

Overall, Spice Garden was a comfortable family restaurant with good service and a wide variety of Indian dishes. The strongest items were pepper chicken, dal makhani, gongura chicken, and gulab jamun. The vegetable biryani was average, and service became slower during the busy period. I would visit again, especially with a group where people want both North Indian and South Indian food.